     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-10-07 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Canada Halts Assisted Dying Expansion for Mental Illness Alone</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Canada</span> <span>#Assisted Dying</span> <span>#Mental Health</span> <span>#Law</span></div>
       <p>Canada has indefinitely suspended plans to expand its medically assisted dying (Maid) law to include individuals whose sole condition is mental illness. This decision, announced on Wednesday, 7 October 2026, means that Canadians with mental illness alone will not be able to access assisted dying by the previously planned date of March 2027. Justice Minister Sean Fraser stated that while there isn't &quot;a perfect consensus on this issue,&quot; the government believes this is &quot;the correct approach at this particular time,&quot; citing a lack of consensus on who would be eligible.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/canada-halts-assisted-dying-expansion-for-mental-illness-alo.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The suspension follows a recommendation earlier this year from a parliamentary panel, which advised against expanding Maid to those with mental illness as their sole underlying medical condition. Despite this, a federal case challenging the government's earlier decision to delay access to Maid for people with mental illness remains before the courts, a matter Fraser acknowledged the Supreme Court could potentially weigh on in the future. In response to the indefinite suspension, the government plans to introduce a new law in the coming weeks. This legislation will not only formalize the indefinite suspension but also propose allowing patients with a progressing incurable illness to consent to assisted dying in advance, before their health deteriorates to the point of incapacitation, though final decisions on these advanced requests would rest with individual provinces.</p><p>Medically assisted dying first became legal in Canada in 2016 for individuals with terminal illnesses, who still constitute the majority of cases. Following a Quebec court case, the law was expanded in 2021 to include people with incurable conditions where death was not imminent, a mechanism known as &quot;track two&quot; Maid. The expansion to include mental illness alone was initially set for 2023 but was delayed twice to allow for further study. Supporters of extending the program argue that not doing so infringes upon constitutional rights and access to healthcare, with figures like Claire Brosseau, who lives with treatment-resistant bipolar 1 disorder, advocating for &quot;a safe death.&quot;</p><p>However, significant concerns have been raised regarding the medical community's understanding of mental illnesses and the appropriateness of determining eligibility for assisted dying based solely on such conditions. Justice Minister Fraser himself noted that a &quot;lack of consensus in the medical community about the irremediable nature of certain mental illnesses… gives me real cause for concern.&quot; Experts have also voiced broader worries that Canada's assisted dying program might be serving to alleviate suffering stemming from inadequate access to affordable housing and healthcare, rather than functioning as a genuine last resort. The parliamentary committee's report earlier this year underscored these points, recommending the indefinite exclusion of persons whose sole underlying medical condition is a mental illness, while also highlighting &quot;a pressing need for increased and more equitable access to adequate mental health services.&quot; The committee's conclusions were informed by experts from countries like the Netherlands and Belgium, which do permit assisted dying for mental illness. While polls indicate a majority of Canadians support access to medically assisted death generally, this support reportedly diminishes when it specifically concerns patients with mental illness alone.</p><p>What to watch: The government will introduce new legislation in the coming weeks.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a comprehensive and accurate summary of the provided source material, effectively covering the suspension details, political context, and expert concerns.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqd09g0gj50ko?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
