     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-07 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Constitutional Conduct Group Criticizes Civil Servants' Independence</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Constitutional Conduct Group (CCG), a collective of former All-India Services officials, has issued an open letter urging serving civil servants to uphold the Constitution. The group alleges that many officials have surrendered their independence to facilitate partisan objectives, noting a decline in democratic dialogue and the frequent labeling of dissent as anti-national.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/constitutional-conduct-group-criticizes-civil-servants-indep.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The letter specifically criticized the Election Commission of India for changes to electoral registration processes, which the CCG claims were designed to disenfranchise voters. Furthermore, the group accused central agencies, including the CBI and the Income-Tax Department, of being complicit in undermining institutional integrity.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the CCG's open letter, though it omits the specific mention of the Environment Ministry, which is a minor detail.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/ccg-urges-serving-civil-servants-to-uphold-constitution-resist-partisan-objectives/article71556580.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
