     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-07 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Deep-Sea Clams Adapt to Fluctuating Energy Sources</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A research team led by The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology has discovered how deep-sea clams maintain energy stability despite fluctuating sulfide levels. By studying Archivesica marissinica at the Haima cold seep, researchers found that metabolic flexibility in bacterial symbionts, combined with host regulation, allows these organisms to adapt to changing environments.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/deep-sea-clams-adapt-to-fluctuating-energy-sources.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study, published in Science Advances, demonstrates a tiered adaptation strategy that sustains the clam holobiont when chemical energy supplies are limited. This research offers critical insights into how chemosynthetic ecosystems remain resilient against environmental shifts caused by geological or chemical fluctuations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately reports the findings regarding deep-sea clam adaptation and the tiered strategy mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-deep-sea-clams-adjust-bacterial.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
