     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-07 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by The Hindu World, BBC World">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>French Students Protest Poor School Conditions, Budget Cuts Amidst Widespread Unrest</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#France</span> <span>#Student Protest</span> <span>#Education</span> <span>#Government</span></div>
       <p>Across France, teenagers have been staging blockades, known as 'blocus,' at the entrances of lycées, or high schools, using trash cans and furniture to protest poor conditions in schools. Over the past two days, police have used force against demonstrators after some protests escalated into violence, with several schools reportedly set ablaze. More than 3,000 people, predominantly students, have been detained so far, including 1,949 on Thursday, October 1, 2026, alone. Approximately 400 schools remained closed on Friday, October 2, 2026, according to The Hindu World.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/french-students-protest-poor-school-conditions-budget-cuts-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Small protests initially began in mid-September in lycées located in the suburbs of Paris, rapidly spreading across the country after information circulated on social media, as confirmed by both The Hindu World and BBC World. Lycées cater to students aged 15 to 18. The Interior Ministry reported that 1,027 out of the country's 3,700 lycées have been affected, with university students also joining, leading to blockades across 30 campuses. These student protests gained momentum and coincided with larger public workers' protests against planned budget cuts by the Emmanuel Macron government ahead of the next year's presidential election. AFP reported that the draft 2027 budget Bill includes plans to cut expenditure by €43 billion to reduce the deficit to 5%. Teachers, particularly from primary schools, and school staff have also joined the demonstrations, demanding better pay.</p><p>Students are primarily protesting against the poor state of schools, including dilapidated buildings, long hours, a shortage of teachers, overcrowded classrooms, and demanding schedules. Slogans such as “More hours of lessons than sleep” and “8 a.m. to 6 p.m.: Are we pupils or under arrest?” have been used. They have also raised grievances about broken toilets and rodents on school premises. A key demand is for equity funding, as schools in lower-income areas reportedly receive significantly less funding compared to those in higher-income regions. Their main demands include increased funds for improving school infrastructure, reduced hours, and the hiring of more teachers.</p><p>Adding to students' frustrations, France experienced one of its hottest summers, with heatwaves pushing temperatures above 40 degrees Celsius in several regions. This has been particularly arduous for students in classrooms often housing over 30 people, with poor insulation and no air conditioning for more than nine hours a day, which has become a major point of contention in their protests.</p><p>In response to the escalating situation, Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu convened a crisis meeting on Thursday, October 1, 2026, and canceled Ministers’ trips to manage the unrest. The Prime Minister’s Office warned against ‘urban violence’ by demonstrators and shifted many schools to online classes. On Wednesday, September 30, 2026, around 85 police officers and gendarmes were injured in clashes. Trash cans were set on fire, and projectiles were thrown at schools by protesters. Two schools in Nantes and Le Havre were reportedly set ablaze, and a teacher was doused in petrol. Municipal tram lines were halted by authorities.</p><p>Riot police deployed less lethal crowd control measures, including tear gas and rubber bullets, resulting in injuries to dozens of protesters, which has drawn criticism from the opposition. Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez acknowledged the escalation of the movement and accused “troublemakers” of inciting violence at different schools. He advised the public to limit travel and deployed additional security forces to maintain law and order. The government, citing intelligence sources, has accused the far-left party La France Insoumise (LFI) of encouraging the protests and causing public disorder. LFI’s presidential candidate, Jean-Luc Mélenchon, stated that the party supports the “non-violent methods of struggle by the students” and accused the government of politicizing the issues raised by the students. Similar protests have also commenced in neighboring Belgium, with authorities ordering schools in Liège to close on Friday, October 2, 2026, according to local media.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is well-structured and incorporates all the key details from the source, including specific dates, figures, and government actions.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 2 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/france-student-protests-over-school-conditions-and-funding/article71536574.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/cr5ynddn318eo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 2 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
    </div>
       
