     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-07 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Madras High Court Reviews Election Petition Against CM Vijay</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Justice V. Lakshminarayanan of the Madras High Court remarked that the election petition challenging Tamil Nadu Chief Minister C. Joseph Vijay’s victory in Perambur has undergone a significant shift due to recent Supreme Court rulings. The court is currently questioning whether alleged discrepancies in the CM’s declared assets, worth over 600 crore rupees, constitute corrupt electoral practices or undue influence on voters.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/madras-high-court-reviews-election-petition-against-cm-vijay.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The judge also questioned how a Returning Officer could be expected to verify the accuracy of complex asset declarations. Petitioner counsel argued that the validity of these declarations, including whether they were made willfully, requires a full trial and the presentation of evidence.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: A concise and accurate summary of the legal proceedings, capturing the judge's shift in perspective regarding Supreme Court precedents.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/entire-texture-of-cm-vijays-election-case-changed-says-madras-high-court-judge/article71556542.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
