     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-07 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NLC Grants R65.6 Million for South Africa's 2027 Cricket World Cup Stadium Upgrades</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket South Africa</span> <span>#NLC</span> <span>#Cricket World Cup 2027</span> <span>#stadium upgrades</span></div>
       <p>The National Lotteries Commission (NLC) has presented Cricket South Africa (CSA) with R65,645,092.30 to fund stadium improvements and new ground equipment. This significant investment is aimed at preparing nine venues across the Western Cape, Eastern Cape, Free State, Gauteng, KwaZulu-Natal and North-West for the ICC Men’s Cricket World Cup 2027. The ceremonial cheque handover took place at Newlands Cricket Ground.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/nlc-grants-r656-million-for-south-africas-2027-cricket-world.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The funding will cover essential permanent stadium infrastructure, broadcast and media facilities, crucial safety improvements, and specialist ground equipment. Among the nine grounds benefiting are all eight South African venues selected to host matches during the 2027 World Cup: Newlands, Boland Park, St George’s Park, Buffalo Park, Mangaung Oval, Kingsmead, SuperSport Park, and the Wanderers Stadium. JB Marks Oval in Potchefstroom will also receive investment.</p><p>Specific planned improvements at Newlands include a new secondary scoreboard, an ICC command centre, upgraded seating, refurbished players’ changing rooms, a new sound and evacuation system, and a new net structure. Buffalo Park in KuGompo City is set to receive a new media centre and broadcasting facility, while Boland Park in Paarl will develop a new area for players and match officials, alongside additional hospitality facilities. All nine venues will also be equipped with specialist items such as mowers, tractors, and pitch rollers.</p><p>CSA Chief Financial Officer Tjaart van der Walt welcomed the NLC investment, emphasizing its importance for both the World Cup and the long-term development of South African cricket. Van der Walt stated, “Every rand has been allocated to a specific, costed need at our venues, from broadcast facilities to seating and safety systems. These upgrades will help us deliver a world-class Cricket World Cup in 2027, and they will serve our players, provinces and supporters long after the final ball is bowled.” He also committed to transparent reporting. National Lotteries Commissioner Jodi Scholtz noted the funding's timely arrival as South Africa prepares to co-host the tournament with Zimbabwe and Namibia, anticipating it will unearth future cricket talent. The ICC Men’s Cricket World Cup 2027 marks the tournament’s return to Africa for the first time since 2003.</p><p>What to watch: South Africa's preparations for the 2027 Cricket World Cup as venue upgrades commence.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft successfully incorporates all key facts, names, figures, and context from the source text, maintaining high accuracy throughout.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/nlc-grants-csa-r656-crore-for-stadium-upgrades-ahead-of-2027-odi-world-cup-2020/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
