     <article class="story single" data-beat="education">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">EDUCATION</span> · Education (aggregate) · 2026-10-07 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>NMC Clarifies Licence Requirement for Georgia Medical Graduates</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#Medical Careers</span> <span>#NMC</span></div>
       <p>The National Medical Commission (NMC) has clarified that medical graduates from Georgia are required to obtain a licence to practice in India. This statement, reported by The Indian Express, addresses a key requirement for foreign medical graduates.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/nmc-clarifies-licence-requirement-for-georgia-medical-gradua.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The NMC explicitly stated that simply holding &quot;junior doctor&quot; status is insufficient for these graduates to practice medicine in India, a matter relevant to the Foreign Medical Graduate Examination (FMGE).</p><p>What to watch: Graduates from Georgia and other foreign countries should ensure they meet all licensing requirements to practice.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately conveys the core mandate from the NMC regarding medical graduates from Georgia, though it is quite brief.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5&amp;hl=en-US&amp;gl=US&amp;ceid=US:en" rel="noopener">Education (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
