     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-07 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Research links water clarity and community engagement in Spermonde coral conservation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Hasanuddin University researchers assessed coral reef health in Indonesia's Spermonde Archipelago using drone surveys and underwater photography. The study, published in Anthropocene Coasts, found that reefs in clearer waters maintain higher live coral cover compared to those in turbid environments. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/research-links-water-clarity-and-community-engagement-in-spe.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Beyond ecological assessment, researchers engaged 33 residents through focus groups to gauge conservation readiness. Findings revealed limited community participation, indicating a critical need for institutional support and training. Researchers suggest that integrating scientific data with local perspectives is essential for developing effective, adaptive management strategies to protect the archipelago's biodiversity against ongoing anthropogenic pressures.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise and accurate reporting of the research findings and community engagement data.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-combining-science-community-knowledge-coral.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
