     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-07 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Russian Strikes Kill 24, Injure 60 Across Ukraine</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#World News</span> <span>#Ukraine</span> <span>#Russia</span> <span>#Conflict</span></div>
       <p>Russia launched a fresh wave of missile and drone attacks across Ukraine overnight, killing at least 24 civilians, including four children, and injuring around 60 others, Ukrainian officials confirmed. The strikes targeted Kyiv and nearly a dozen other Ukrainian regions, causing significant damage, with residential buildings among the sites hit.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/russian-strikes-kill-24-injure-60-across-ukraine.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>One Russian missile struck a residential building, directly leading to the deaths of the four children. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy specified that three of the children were just one year old, while the fourth was eight. Ukraine's intelligence agencies had detected preparations for a large-scale assault a day earlier, as noted by Zelenskyy, yet air defences were unable to prevent the heavy casualties and damage.</p><p>These latest attacks underscore the persistent challenges Ukraine faces in defending against Russia’s long-range weapons. Kyiv is particularly struggling to intercept ballistic missiles and a newer type of jet-powered drone that Moscow has increasingly deployed. The country's ability to counter Russian ballistic weapons is also hampered by shortages of ammunition for its US-made Patriot air defence systems, though Kyiv is working to develop new interceptors for faster, high-altitude jet-powered drones.</p><p>The overnight strikes occurred as Russia and Ukraine continue a wider long-range aerial conflict, with ground forces largely locked in a stalemate. More than four and a half years after Russia's full-scale invasion, repeated aerial attacks are causing growing damage on both sides, though the impact is considered far more severe for Ukraine due to its smaller resources. Efforts led by the United States to push for peace have offered little hope, with discussions about low-level technical talks remaining ongoing. Western analysts suggest Russian President Vladimir Putin continues the war, believing sustained pressure could force Ukraine to yield and weaken Western allies' resolve.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing aerial conflict and international efforts to bolster Ukraine's air defence capabilities.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, well-structured, and incorporates all key details provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
