       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-07 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Scientists Outline Five Steps to Mitigate El Niño Health Risks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Climate researchers from the University of Bristol have identified five essential actions to prepare for the impending super El Niño and reduce its impact on human health. Recommended measures include strengthening global protective responses, supporting national governments with early warning systems, scaling up climate-health attribution research, continuously reviewing health impacts, and assessing the cost-effectiveness of mitigation strategies.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/scientists-outline-five-steps-to-mitigate-el-nino-health-ris.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Experts emphasize that the El Niño cycle, which exacerbates risks like malnutrition, heatstroke, and infectious diseases, is predictable months in advance. The study urges collaboration between the World Health Organization, international bodies, and national governments to address evidence gaps and protect vulnerable populations from the cycle's evolving health consequences.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief concisely covers all five points and the context provided in the source text.</p>
