     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-10-07 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>World’s largest penguin colony suffers ‘enormous population crash’</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Penguins</span> <span>#Antarctica</span> <span>#Climate Crisis</span></div>
       <p>The world’s largest chinstrap penguin colony, located on Zavodovski Island in Antarctica, has experienced an &quot;enormous population crash,&quot; with numbers declining by two-thirds since 2011. Scientists attribute this significant reduction primarily to the climate crisis. Zavodovski, the northernmost of the South Sandwich Islands, housed an estimated 600,000 chinstrap penguins and 90,000 macaroni penguins in 2011, but by 2025, it is likely to have lost its title as the biggest colony to Cape Adare in the Ross Sea. Prof. Norman Ratcliffe, a penguin ecologist at the British Antarctic Survey and leader of the research, quantified the loss as equivalent to half a million breeding individuals from that island.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-07/worlds-largest-penguin-colony-suffers-enormous-population-cr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The rapid demise of chinstrap penguins, as detailed in a new study published Wednesday in Current Biology, has prompted warnings from scientists about an increased risk of a wider ecosystem collapse. Chinstrap penguins are particularly vulnerable due to their specialized diets and specific ocean temperature preferences. This vulnerability is exacerbated by human-driven heating, which has seen winter temperatures on and around the Antarctic peninsula rise by 5C above preindustrial levels, making it one of the fastest warming regions globally. An extreme example occurred on June 6, when the Argentinian Esperanza base on the Trinity peninsula recorded a winter high of 15.4C, approximately 20C above normal for that time of year, a margin scientists described as &quot;absolutely crazy.&quot;</p><p>Tracking data indicates that chinstrap penguins typically forage in cooler waters to the north-west of Zavodovski Island, where Antarctic krill, their primary food source, are abundant. However, as ocean temperatures warm, the krill have migrated south into the Weddell Sea, disrupting the penguins' feeding patterns. While macaroni penguins, being less selective eaters, saw their numbers rise by 30%, this increase is insufficient to offset the substantial loss of chinstraps. Ratcliffe expressed concern that if current trends persist, all penguin species will eventually face a shortage of suitable habitat. He likened the ecosystem to a &quot;giant Jenga set,&quot; where the removal of each component heightens the risk of complete collapse.</p><p>Researchers ruled out several other potential causes for the decline, including competition for food between chinstrap and macaroni penguins and human impact, given the nearest human settlements are thousands of miles away. Although Zavodovski Island is volcanic and experienced a major eruption in 2016, leading to a &quot;Penguin Pompeii hypothesis,&quot; data showed a steady linear decline rather than a sudden fall. Observations also confirmed that chinstraps avoided the main areas affected by ash and lava deposition.</p><p>What to watch: Ongoing research into Antarctic climate impacts on marine ecosystems.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft comprehensively covers the scientific study, the ecological context, and the specific details regarding the population decline.</p>
